course: dictionary
lesson: 4873
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word idea can mean several things, and the words around it tell you which one is meant. «That's a good idea» is about a plan or thought. «I have no idea» is about what you know. Look at the whole sentence before you choose a meaning.
explain: The main idea is the big point of a text, the thing it is mostly about. When your teacher asks for the main idea of a paragraph, they want one central point, not every small detail. Ask yourself: what is this whole paragraph really saying?
explain: Some phrases use idea in a fixed way, such as have an idea, get the idea, bright idea and the idea of. Read the whole phrase together, because its meaning is not always the same as the single words added up.
fact: An idea is a thought or plan that comes into your mind, especially a new one.
fact: «I have no idea» means you do not know.
fact: «The idea of the game is to score the most goals» means the main point or purpose of the game.
fact: «She has her own ideas about it» means she has her own opinion or belief.
fact: In science, an idea that you test is called a hypothesis.
exercise: Dad smiles and says «That's not the worst idea you've had», then says «let's do it». What does he mean? | He wants to stop the plan | It is the worst idea anyone has had | * It is a good idea | He has no idea what you mean
exercise: «I have no idea where my pencil went.» What does idea mean here? | An opinion about pencils | A new plan for a game | The main point of a story | * What you know: I do not know
exercise: «The idea of the game is to score the most goals.» What is the idea of the game? | * Its main point or purpose | A lucky guess | A sound the ball makes | Its rules for the referee
test: «She has her own ideas about it.» What does ideas mean here? | A recipe for dinner | Something she has lost | A plan for a party | * An opinion or belief
test: Charles Darwin worked for over 20 years before publishing his big idea in 1859. What does idea mean here? | A cake recipe | A holiday he wanted to take | * A major new thought he had worked out over time | Something he had forgotten
exam: Jayden says «I get the idea now.» What does he mean? | He has a picture of a dog | * He understands what is meant | He has forgotten the idea | He has a plan for a trip
